'''Narungga''' (also '''Narangga''') is an [[Australian Aboriginal languages|Australian Aboriginal language]] spoken by the [[Narungga people]] in [[Yorke Peninsula]], [[South Australia]]. As a result of the [[History of Australia (1788&ndash;1850)|colonisation of Australia]], the Narungga language [[language death|fell into disuse]] within several generations. Nevertheless, Narungga continued to be [[language documentation|documented]] into the 20th century, and the 1980s saw a community reclamation. As a result of [[language revival|revival]] efforts, the language along with Narungga culture is now being taught around the [[Yorke Peninsula]], from [[Moonta, South Australia|Moonta]] and [[Maitland, South Australia|Maitland]] Area Schools to [[Point Pearce]].

Narungga is one of the languages in the [[Yura languages|Yura]] group, which includes [[Nukunu language|Nukunu]], [[Kaurna language|Kaurna]], and [[Ngadjuri language|Ngadjuri]], among others, and belongs to the [[Pama–Nyungan languages|Pama–Nyungan]] family.

